This Python implementation of `MinStack` uses an auxiliary stack (`min_stack`) to keep track of the minimum elements. The `push` method adds an element to both the main stack and the `min_stack` if it's smaller than or equal to the current minimum. The `pop` method removes the top element from both stacks if it matches the current minimum. The `top` method simply returns the top element of the main stack, and `get_min` returns the top element of the `min_stack`, which represents the current minimum in the stack. This ensures that all operations are performed in constant time, O(1).
